# Line endings are a CORRECTNESS issue, not a style preference.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its SYSTEM config, so a Windows clone
# silently rewrites text files to CRLF. On a Windows box whose checkout lives inside a
# WSL2 distro, containers read those same files, and a shell script with CRLF does not
# fail gracefully:
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# Pinning eol=lf here overrides autocrlf for every developer, every tool and every OS,
# because .gitattributes travels with the repo. That is the only layer that protects a
# clone made by something nobody anticipated: measured 2026-09-11, the WebNesting VS Code
# extension's own Clone Repository command - it runs host-side, so it shells out to
# Windows git - produced a CRLF worktree inside the distro, and GitHub Desktop reported
# that repo CLEAN because its git normalises on read and so hid its own damage, while VS
# Code in the Remote-WSL window correctly showed 8 modified files.
#
# The machine-level belt for this is `core.autocrlf=false` in the host's global git
# config, set by system/windows/bootstrap.ps1. This file is the braces, and it is the half
# that still works for a developer who never ran that bootstrap.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-side helpers keep CRLF, which is what PowerShell and Notepad expect. Listed
# even where this repo has none today, so the rule is already correct if it gains one.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
